|# neutron-zzz6-dwrite-fontface-keepalive
|#
|# Keep the N most recently used IDWriteFontFace objects alive so their RASTERISED GLYPH CACHES
|# survive, instead of dying with the face and being re-rasterised through FreeType.
|# Touches: dlls/dwrite/main.c, dlls/dwrite/font.c.
|#
|# ORDERING: zzz6 -- applies after neutron-zzz5-dwrite-parsed-font-cache, whose factory-level
|# cache it sits beside and mirrors in shape.
|#
|# THE PROBLEM
|# dwrite's glyph cache lives on the fontface. The factory's fontface cache tracks faces but holds
|# NO REFERENCE -- a face unlinks itself when destroyed. Code that builds text layouts per frame
|# therefore destroys and recreates faces constantly, and every new face starts with an EMPTY
|# glyph cache.
|#
|# MEASURED (preserved-fixes/harnesses/glyphbench.c -- 5 text formats, 10 timeline-like strings,
|# 40 frames), before -> after:
|#   fontfaces created   2189  ->     1
|#   glyph cache misses 39,824 ->   318
|#   hit rate              66% ->   99%
|#   ms/frame (best)      2.14 ->  1.47      (-31%)
|#
|# Motivated by a CPU profile of Premiere's timeline zoom (2026-07-30, runtime 11.10-16) which put
|# ~19.6% of the UI thread in font rasterisation: libfreetype 8.98% plus another 10.6% of libc
|# called FROM libfreetype (its allocation traffic).
|#
|# ⚠️ THE OBVIOUS FIX WAS WRONG, AND THIS IS WHY THE INSTRUMENTATION SHIPS WITH IT.
|# The code carried a long comment arguing the 32 KB glyph cache was far too small and should be
|# "a few MB". It is not the constraint: a sweep from 32 KB to 4 MB moved nothing (2.06-2.16
|# ms/frame), because with faces dying constantly the cache never filled -- peak 3 KB, ZERO
|# evictions. Capacity was never the problem; object LIFETIME was. Raising the cap would have been
|# a no-op that looked like progress. Only after this patch does the cache actually fill (peak
|# 32 KB), and even then a re-sweep shows no further gain, so the default is left ALONE.
|# `NEUTRON_DWRITE_GLYPH_CACHE_KB` exists to re-measure that in a real app rather than guess.
|#
|# WHY NOT SHARE ONE GLYPH CACHE AT FACTORY LEVEL (the "obvious" bigger fix):
|# cache entries are MUTATED after lookup (entry->advance, entry->bitmap) under the caller's
|# fontface->cs. Sharing one cache across faces would require restructuring that locking across
|# three hot call sites in a DLL every Adobe app loads -- reopening the freeze/deadlock class this
|# project has spent months closing. Keeping the face alive gets the same glyph reuse and changes
|# NO locking: each face still owns its cache under its own cs.
|#
|# BOUNDED at 24 faces. The evicted face is released OUTSIDE factory->cs, because a face's
|# destructor takes that same lock to unlink itself from the fontface cache -- releasing the last
|# reference while holding it would self-deadlock.
|#
|# Also included: TRACE-gated counters on the `nglyph` channel (hits/misses/evictions/peak, plus
|# faces created/cleared). They are what proved the size hypothesis wrong and identified lifetime
|# as the real mechanism; a statistic that never prints is not a measurement.
|diff --git a/dlls/dwrite/font.c b/dlls/dwrite/font.c
|index d33bc61c70e..4457568d5a3 100644
|#
|# MEASURED IN PREMIERE (30 s zoom, GPU mode, one build A/B'd via NEUTRON_DWRITE_FACE_KEEPALIVE):
|#   fontfaces created  88,980 -> 5      (~3,000 faces/sec created while zooming)
|#   glyph cache misses 587,522 -> 424   (63% -> 99% hit)
|#   evictions               0 -> 0      peak 13 KB -> 17 KB of a 32 KB cap
|# User-confirmed the keepalive-OFF arm was 'a little choppier' (test was NOT blind --
|# corroboration, not proof; the counters are the evidence).
|# The synthetic benchmark understated the churn by 40x (2189 vs 88,980) -- it got the
|# mechanism right and the magnitude badly wrong. Confirm synthetics in the real app.
|--- a/dlls/dwrite/main.c
|+++ b/dlls/dwrite/main.c
--------------------------
